Why: mainline read-modify-writes DMAC_CFG - the single controller-wide register
holding DMAC_EN (bit 0) and INT_EN (bit 1) - from two contexts with nothing
serializing them. dw_axi_dma_interrupt() clears INT_EN on entry and restores it
on exit; axi_chan_block_xfer_start() sets DMAC_EN on every channel start. The
per-channel chan->vc.lock does not cover the handler's writes, so on SMP the two
interleave and one update is lost. The handler's completion callback wakes the
submitting thread on the other CPU while INT_EN is still 0, which is the exact
window that makes the bad interleave likely rather than theoretical: that thread
reads DMAC_CFG with INT_EN clear, and its write-back of DMAC_EN lands after the
handler has restored INT_EN, clearing it again.

Nothing sets INT_EN except the handler's own exit path, so the loss is
self-latching. The controller stays enabled and keeps completing transfers, and
per-channel INTSTATUS keeps being set, but no interrupt ever reaches the GIC
again: every dmaengine client blocks until its timeout, forever, controller-wide,
with no driver message of any kind.

Seen twice after ~200k interrupts of a 60 fps memcpy client. Live, in the failed
state: DMAC_CFG 0x00000001 (DMAC_EN set, INT_EN clear), DMAC_INTSTATUS 0x00000003
(channels 0 and 1 pending), dw_axi_dmac IRQ counts frozen, dmesg clean. Writing
INT_EN back by hand restored the engine on the spot - the client went from
4091.6 ms per batch (four 1 s dmaengine timeouts) back to 16.6 ms with no restart.

Fix is a leaf spinlock (chip->cfg_lock, added by 0500) around each of the four
DMAC_CFG helpers. None of them calls another, so there is nothing to nest.

A second, unrelated defect in the same file: axi_chan_handle_err() logs the bad
descriptor and then calls vchan_cookie_complete(), the identical completion the
success path takes. The callback runs and the cookie completes with nothing
carrying the fact that the engine refused the transfer, so a client waiting on
that callback treats a copy that never ran as done and consumes whatever the
destination held.

virt-dma already carries a per-descriptor dmaengine_result and hands it to a
callback_result client, so the fix is to set it: DMA_TRANS_ABORTED before the
completion, which is what lets such a client have a fallback path at all.

A third change, independent of both: batched memcpy on one channel enable.

The dmaengine API prepares one descriptor per memcpy. A client with several
copies to make therefore submits several descriptors, and the controller enables
a channel once per copy. On this SoC that enable is a hazard: a channel
re-enabled within microseconds of its own completion, while the display
controller is fetching from the same DDR, wedges the memory fabric. The SoC stops
dead with the last frame on the panel, no printk, no ping, and recovers only
through the watchdog reset ~35 s later. Measured at 2.48 hangs per hour with the
compose blit's three plane copies spread over two channels, which puts two of the
three copies on channel 0 microseconds apart.

The vendor never had this shape. Its fusion compositor builds one descriptor
holding up to 128 src/dst/len triples and issues a single ioctl on /dev/axi_dma,
so the engine walks the whole list in hardware on one enable.

The hardware supports exactly that here too: every axi_dma_lli carries its own
sar, dar, block_ts and llp, and this driver already chains LLIs when one copy
exceeds the channel block size. The change is to let the chain cross from one
copy to the next.

dma_chan_prep_dma_memcpy()'s block-filling loop moves into axi_desc_fill_memcpy(),
which appends one copy's blocks at a caller-held index.
dw_axi_dma_prep_memcpy_batch() calls it once per entry and returns the single
descriptor covering all of them; it is exported for the out-of-tree ml_dmablit
module, which is the compositor's path to this engine.
dma_chan_prep_dma_memcpy() becomes a one-entry call into it, so the standard
dmaengine path and the batch path build identical chains.

Serialising three plane copies on one channel costs about the duration of the
middle plane, roughly +250 us on a measured 1471 us tile, against a 16.6 ms
frame.

One behaviour change comes with the split: nr_hw_descs is now the number of LLI
blocks actually taken rather than the pre-computed upper bound, so a descriptor
that fails part way through frees exactly the blocks it took. Mainline frees the
bound, which hands dma_pool_free() a NULL lli for every block it never reached
and logs "bad dma" for each. The bound can also exceed the real count, because
the transfer width is recomputed per block and can grow as the addresses advance.

